<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-percentages" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Fractions and Percentages</title>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-interpret">
  <title>Converting Fractions</title>
  

<p> Fractions greater than one come in two forms: </p>
<p>
<ul>
<li>
Improper Fractions have a greater numerator than denominator, for example <m>\frac{7}{6}</m>.
</li>
<li>
Mixed Fractions identify the greatest integer that is less than the fraction, and the remainder, for example  <m>1\frac{1}{6}</m>.
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>Improper fractions are commonly preferred for calculations, but to interpret a fraction, writing it in mixed form is useful. 
We need to be able to convert between the forms. </p>

  <example>
    <title>Converting to a Mixed Fraction</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Write <m>\frac{37}{7}</m> as a mixed fraction.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We first ask 'How many times does 7 go into 37?' Now <m>5\times 7=35</m> and <m>6\times 7=42</m> which is too big, 
        so the answer is '5 times'. This is the integer in our mixed fraction. 
      </p>
      <p>
        The remainder is <m>37-35=2</m>, and so 
        <md>\frac{37}{7}=5\frac{2}{7}.</md>
      </p>
    
    </solution>
  </example>

  <exercise label="ww-fraction-convert">
    <title>Converting to a Mixed Fraction</title>
    <webwork>
        <pg-code>
            # Set the math environment to expect fraction format answers
            Context("Fraction-NoDecimals");
            
            # Define the exact answer
            $int = 4;
            $frac = Fraction(7,9);
        </pg-code>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Write <m>\frac{43}{9}</m> as a mixed fraction.
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>\frac{43}{9} = </m> <var name="$int" width="10"/><var name="$frac" width="10"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </webwork>
</exercise>



  <example>
    <title>Converting to an Improper Fraction</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Write <m>3\frac{6}{11}</m> as an improper fraction.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Here we have 3 as our integer. Since we are working with elevenths, <m>1=\frac{11}{11}</m>. Then <m>3=3\left(\frac{11}{11}\right)=\frac{33}{11}</m>.
        We have an additional <m>\frac{6}{11}</m>, and so
        <md>3\frac{6}{11}=\frac{33+6}{11}=\frac{39}{11}.</md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <exercise label="ww-improper-convert">
    <title>Converting to an Improper Fraction</title>
    <webwork>
        <pg-code>
            # Set the math environment to expect fraction format answers
            Context("Fraction-NoDecimals");
            
            # Define the exact answer
            $ans = Fraction(37,5);
        </pg-code>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Write <m>7\frac{2}{5}</m> as an improper fraction.
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>7\frac{2}{5} = </m> <var name="$ans" width="10"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </webwork>
</exercise>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-reduce">
  <title>Reducing Fractions</title>

  <p>
    Where possible we want to simplify fractions. Fractions where the numerator and denominator have no common factors (other than 1 and -1) are
    said to be in reduced or lowest form . For example, <m>\frac{5}{6}</m> is in reduced form, since 5 and 6 have no common factors. On the other hand,
    <m>\frac{4}{6}</m> is not in reduced form, since 4 and 6 are both divisible by 2 (they have a common factor of 2). 
  </p>

<example>
    <title>Reducing Fractions</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Write <m>\frac{30}{35}</m> in reduced form.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Both 30 and 35 are divisible by 5. In particular <m>30=6\times 5</m> and <m>35=7\times 5</m>. We can cancel out the common factor of 5 to simplify the fraction.
        <md>\frac{30}{35}=\frac{6\times 5}{7\times 5}=\frac{6}{7}.</md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>


  <exercise label="ww-fraction-reduce">
    <title>Reducing Fractions</title>
    <webwork>
        <pg-code>
            # Set the math environment to expect fraction format answers
            Context("Fraction-NoDecimals");
            
            # Define the exact answer
            $ans = Fraction(20,21);
        </pg-code>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Write <m>\frac{60}{63}</m> in reduced form.
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>\frac{60}{63} = </m> <var name="$ans" width="10"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </webwork>
</exercise>

<p>If you find it difficult to identify common factors, see <xref ref="sec-prime"/></p>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-percent">
  <title>Calculating Percentages</title>

  <p>
    Here we introduce a trick for calculating percentages in certain cases. 
  </p>

<example>
    <title>Calculating Percentages</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Calculate 62% of 150. 
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        This seems quite challenging at first, since 62 isn't a particularly nice percentage to work with. However, 150 is, and 62% of 150 is the same as 150% of 62. 
        The latter is much easier to calculate, since 50% of 62 is 31. Then 150% of 62 is 62+31=93. Thus 62% of 150 is also 93. 
      </p>
      <p> In general, if a percentage calculation seems difficult, it's worth considering if swapping the numbers would make it easier. </p>
    </solution>
  </example>


  <exercise label="ww-percent">
    <title>Calculating Percentages</title>
    <webwork>
        <pg-code>
            # Set the math environment to expect fraction format answers
            Context("Fraction-NoDecimals");
            
            # Define the exact answer
            $ans = 27;
        </pg-code>
        <statement>
            <p>
                36% of 75 is <var name="$ans" width="10"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </webwork>
</exercise>

<p>If you find it difficult to identify common factors, see <xref ref="sec-prime"/></p>

</subsection>


